# These files are checked out on Windows and then copied to a Linux Pi, where
# a CRLF is fatal rather than cosmetic:
#
#   * a shell script with CRLF fails with `$'\r': command not found`
#   * a systemd unit with CRLF puts a \r inside the ExecStart command line, so
#     bash -lc receives a trailing carriage return and the whole unit dies at
#     start with a message that names no obvious cause
#   * a params YAML with CRLF can still parse, but the trailing \r ends up
#     INSIDE the topics_glob string, so the last entry in the whitelist stops
#     matching. A safety whitelist that silently loses its last entry is worse
#     than no whitelist, because it looks correct in `cat`.
#   * console.html is served verbatim to a browser; CRLF is harmless there but
#     pinned anyway so a diff of the UI is never a diff of line endings
#
# So: force LF regardless of core.autocrlf on whoever's machine.
*.sh          text eol=lf
*.service     text eol=lf
*.yaml        text eol=lf
*.py          text eol=lf
*.md          text eol=lf
*.html        text eol=lf

# Batch files are the exception. cmd.exe wants CRLF and misparses a bare LF in
# some constructs, and this one never leaves Windows.
*.cmd         text eol=crlf
*.ps1         text eol=crlf
